<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Ben-Gurion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as David Grün</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David Ben-Gurion was the Zionist labour leader who declared Israel's independence on 14 May 1948 and served as its first prime minister, leading the state through the 1948 war, the mass immigration that doubled its population, and the building of its institutions and army. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, he had headed the Jewish Agency under Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, and after leaving office he retired to a kibbutz in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a>; he remains Israel's founding statesman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion.jpg" alt="David Ben-Gurion" width="330" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Cohen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Ben-Gurion_(D597-087).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>David Grün was born on 16 October 1886 in Płońsk, in Congress Poland, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. His father, Avigdor Grün, was a court scribe and unofficial legal adviser who co-founded a Zionist group, Beni Zion, after the publication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a>'s Der Judenstaat in 1896. His mother died in 1897. His formal education ended after his bar mitzvah, and at fourteen he and two friends formed a youth club, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a>, which promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> studies and emigration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>.</p><p>He landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> on 7 September 1906 and worked as a day labourer at Petah Tikva, where he caught malaria. He adopted the name Ben-Gurion in 1909. Deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by the Ottoman authorities in March 1915, he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where a speaking tour to raise a Jewish pioneer force produced few recruits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919 Ben-Gurion led the centrist faction of the Labor Zionist movement in forming Ahdut HaAvoda. He helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histadrut">Histadrut</a>, the Zionist labour federation, in 1920 and was its general secretary from 1921 to 1935. After the British White Paper of 1939 limited Jewish immigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, he changed his policy towards the British and began preparing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a> for war, while also urging support for the British war effort. He became executive head of the World Zionist Organization in 1946.</p><p>On 14 May 1948, the last day of the British Mandate, he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel">State of Israel</a> and was the first to sign its Declaration of Independence. During the 1948 war he ordered the militias merged into the Israel Defense Forces; in the Altalena Affair he had the Irgun's arms ship shelled, and sixteen Irgun fighters and three soldiers died. The war involved the expulsion and flight of most of the Palestinian Arab population, and historians disagree about his part in it. Benny Morris holds that he created an atmosphere of transfer without written orders.</p><p>Elected prime minister after the election of 14 February 1949, he held office until 1963 except for 1954 and 1955, when Moshe Sharett led the government. He oversaw the airlift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Arab countries, the National Water Carrier and settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a>, and he backed Israel's invasion of the Sinai in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956. He also pursued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ben-Gurion retired from politics in 1970 and lived in a modest hut at the kibbutz of Sde Boker, writing an eleven-volume history of Israel's early years. He suffered a cerebral haemorrhage on 18 November 1973 and died on 1 December. He is buried beside his wife Paula at Midreshet Ben-Gurion. Israel's main airport and Ben-Gurion University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> bear his name, and his hut is now a visitors' centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David Ben-Gurion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 14 May the last High Commissioner, General Sir Alan Cunningham, left Jerusalem, and that afternoon <b>David Ben-Gurion</b> declared the State of Israel, effective at midnight, when the Mandate expired.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>David Ben-Gurion</b>, secretary general of the Histadrut and leader of the Zionist labour movement, declared the State of Israel; Labor Zionism was also shaped by Nachman Syrkin, Berl Katznelson, Ber Borochov and A. D. Gordon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>After the United Nations voted to partition Palestine in 1947, <b>David Ben-Gurion</b> declared independence on 14 May 1948; the war that followed secured the new state against the armies of its neighbours, while some 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were driven from their homes, remembered as the Nakba.</blockquote><a href="/state/israel">Israel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Płońsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.62,20.38&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Płońsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of David Ben-Gurion"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4503 5593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4503 5593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.62,20.38&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
